tier: strong
branch: feat/first-run-ux-polish
depends_on:
owns: src/charon/cli.py, src/charon/proxy_server.py, src/charon/gateway.py, src/charon/connect.py
prompt: /home/stack/charon-private/fleet/prompts/ux-polish.md
scope: PRODUCTION-READINESS. COLLECTOR ticket — a batch of small first-run / `charon setup`
  UX-polish nits surfaced during dogfooding, batched here so we DON'T spawn one ticket per nit.
  Each item is a self-contained, low-risk cosmetic improvement to the first-run experience.
  Items (growable list):
  1. **Colorize the provider presets in `charon setup`.** During the interactive setup wizard,
     the list of preset/known providers the user picks from does not visually stand out, so users
     scroll past it (operator dogfood observation, charon-vm 2026-06-27). Apply terminal
     color/emphasis (ANSI) so the provider presets clearly stand out, with a no-color fallback
     (honour `NO_COLOR`, and degrade gracefully on a non-TTY / dumb terminal). Keep it
     product-clean and agnostic.

  2. **[correctness, HIGH] *** HIGHEST-VALUE ITEM *** Validate the API key at setup.**
     (operator dogfood, charon-vm 2026-06-27.) `charon setup` stores the key via getpass but
     never checks it works. A provider's `/models` endpoint is often PUBLIC (e.g. opencode-zen),
     so a bad/doubled key still imports the catalog fine and setup reports success — the failure
     only surfaces much later as a cryptic upstream `AuthError: Invalid API key` on the first
     chat request. Setup (and/or `providers add`) should do a quick auth probe right after
     storing the key (a minimal authenticated call / reuse `providers test`) and clearly flag a
     bad key immediately. This would have caught the operator's doubled-paste key at setup
     instead of at first request. NOTE: this is the highest-value item in this collector — it is a
     CORRECTNESS gap (turns a setup mistake into a confusing runtime error), not a cosmetic nit.
     On activation, consider whether it belongs in its own correctness ticket rather than batched
     with cosmetic polish.

  3. **[UX] getpass key prompt gives zero paste feedback.** (charon-vm 2026-06-27.) The masked
     field shows nothing on paste, which led the operator to paste twice (corrupting the key).
     After storing, echo a masked confirmation like `✓ key stored (…<last4>)` so the user knows
     it landed (and roughly that it's the right length).

  4. **[UX] "Start the gateway" hint hardcodes `charon`.** (charon-vm 2026-06-27.) The
     end-of-setup message prints `Start the gateway: charon gateway`, but on installs where
     `charon` isn't on PATH (venv installs, fresh pipx before shell reload) that fails with
     `command not found`. Print the ACTUAL invocation path (`sys.argv[0]`) instead of a bare
     `charon`, everywhere the code suggests a `charon …` command.

  5. **[UX] Gateway startup console URL is misleading when remote/loopback.** (charon-vm
     2026-06-27.) On a loopback bind the startup line prints `console: http://127.0.0.1:8080/`,
     which only works ON that host — confusing when the user is remote. When bound to loopback,
     hint it's local-only; when bound to `0.0.0.0`, show the LAN-reachable form (host IP) so users
     know what to point a browser at.

  6. **[minor] Editing `~/.charon/secrets.json` requires a gateway restart** to take effect
     (charon-vm 2026-06-27) — config is loaded at startup, not per-request. Low priority — note
     for docs, or consider hot-reload later.

  7. **[docs] Docker prerequisite — user not in the `docker` group.** First-run on 4-lom hit
     `permission denied … /var/run/docker.sock` because the user wasn't in the `docker` group.
     `docs/docker.md` should note: add the user to the `docker` group
     (`sudo usermod -aG docker $USER` + re-login) or use `sudo`. (charon-vm/4-lom dogfood
     2026-06-27.)

  8. **[docs, IMPORTANT] gateway-vs-orchestrator clarity in onboarding.** New users conflate
     Charon's two modes. Onboarding/README must clearly state: (A) GATEWAY mode — Charon is *only
     the LLM backend*; the user points their own AGENT (opencode/Cline/aider) at their OWN repo,
     and Charon never touches the repo or tickets. (B) ORCHESTRATOR mode (`charon work` /
     `charon intake`) — the user points Charon ITSELF at a `--repo` and feeds it work via
     `charon intake import`. A brand-new user has neither our repos nor "tickets" — they bring
     their own. Flag as a docs/onboarding priority (bigger than a cosmetic nit). (charon-vm/4-lom
     dogfood 2026-06-27.)

  9. **[discoverability + UX] Web setup page is hidden / not linked.** The gateway serves an
     editable web SETUP page at `/charon/setup` (read-write: add providers/keys/models/pools/tiers,
     hot-reloads), but the read-only dashboard at `/` does NOT link to it — the operator ran the
     gateway for a whole session without realizing the setup page existed (only saw `/`). Fix:
     (1) add a visible **⚙ Setup** button/link on the `/` (and `/charon`) dashboard that goes to
     `/charon/setup`, and a "← Dashboard" link back from setup; (2) propagate the `?token=…`
     through these links (the page already has the token) so the user doesn't have to re-paste it.
     This makes the web-config surface discoverable. (operator dogfood 2026-06-27)

  10. **[UX] Token-in-URL is clunky.** When the gateway is network-exposed, every `/charon*` GET
     needs `?token=…` in the URL (the operator asked "why is the token required? never seen this
     elsewhere" — the answer is sound: the setup page holds API keys + the gateway spends money,
     so it must auth when LAN-reachable). Smooth the UX: let the user enter the token ONCE (a small
     login field or the first `?token=`) and set a short-lived **cookie/session**, so subsequent
     navigation doesn't carry `?token=` in the URL. Keep the existing CSRF/Origin/Sec-Fetch guards.
     (Security rationale stays; this is purely the auth-UX ergonomics.)

  CROSS-REF: "show the live model list at the model-selection prompt" is NOT tracked here — it is
  tracked as **Phase A of TIER-RECS** (`board/TIER-RECS.md.parked`), so it isn't double-tracked.
  Keep UX-POLISH for purely cosmetic nits (e.g. the colorize-presets item above).

  CROSS-REF: the colorize-presets item (1) moved into ACTIVE ticket SETUP-UX-A
  (`board/SETUP-UX-A.md`, opus, `feat/setup-ux-a`) — it bundles there with the two
  TIER-RECS Phase A setup-UX fixes since all three live in `_cmd_setup` (one file). Keep
  UX-POLISH for any FUTURE purely-cosmetic first-run nits.

  Additional first-run UX nits get appended here as dogfooding surfaces them.
  CONSTRAINTS: product-clean (no SLOP/fleet/rig leak), agent/provider-agnostic, stdlib-only.
note: PARKED. First-run UX polish batch (production-readiness). Low-risk, cosmetic; activate
  opportunistically. Append new dogfood-surfaced UX nits as items rather than spawning per-nit
  tickets.

